.TH CGTK-THEME 1 "November 2026" "CGTK 1.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cgtk-theme \- choose and edit the theme of CGTK programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cgtk-theme
.RB [ \-\-dark | \-\-light ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR font ]
.RI [ theme ]
.br
.B cgtk-theme \-l
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cgtk-theme
chooses the theme and the font that programs built with CGTK use, and
edits themes.
.PP
With no arguments it opens a window. The window shows the theme being
edited on itself, and its preview shows a little of every kind of
widget a theme draws: menus, a toolbar, buttons and a default button,
check and radio buttons, fields, a notebook with a list, text and
scales, a progress bar, a scrollbar, a sunken and a raised frame either
side of a pane's handle, with a tree and arrows in them, and a status
bar, with some of them disabled. Choose a theme from the list,
and with
.B Dark
its dark version, then change its
colours on the Colours page, the font on the Font page, and what its
engine offers on the Options page. The six colours at the top of the
Colours page take the others of their kind with them; below them are
all twenty, each on its own. With "Apply as you edit", running CGTK programs follow each
change a moment after it is made; otherwise
.B Apply
makes them follow.
.PP
Themes installed with the toolkit are never changed. Editing one makes
a copy of it among your own themes, named for it ("Altus Custom"), or
under the name given with
.BR "Save As" .
Your own themes are changed in place, and can be deleted.
.PP
A theme
.I Foo
and a theme
.I Foo-Dark
are the light and dark versions of one theme, and the list shows them as
one. The
.B Dark
check box chooses between them, and stays as it was set when another
theme is chosen, for as long as that theme has a dark version too. Every
theme installed with the toolkit has one. Editing an installed dark
version makes a copy called
.IR "Foo Custom-Dark" ,
the dark version of a copy of
.IR Foo .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I theme
Use
.IR theme :
the name of a theme, or the directory of one.
.TP
.B \-\-dark
Use the dark version of
.IR theme ,
or of the theme in use.
.TP
.B \-\-light
Use the light version of
.IR theme ,
or of the theme in use.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-font " \fIfont\fR"
Use
.I font
in every theme, named as "Sans 10" or "DejaVu Serif Bold 12". An empty
.I font
goes back to each theme's own.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List the themes there are, marking the one in use.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options.
.PP
Running programs are told of a new theme or font as soon as it is set.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gtk-1.0/gtkrc
The rc file every CGTK program reads, in
.I ~/.config
unless
.I XDG_CONFIG_HOME
says otherwise.
.B cgtk-theme
writes it: it includes the theme chosen, sets the font if one was
chosen, and includes
.I gtkrc.mine
beside it, where settings of your own belong.
.TP
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/themes/ name /gtk-1.4/gtkrc
A theme of your own, in
.I ~/.local/share
unless
.I XDG_DATA_HOME
says otherwise.
.TP
.IR datadir /themes/ name /gtk-1.4/gtkrc
A theme installed with the toolkit. A theme written for GTK+ 1.2, with
.I gtk/gtkrc
in place of
.IR gtk-1.4/gtkrc ,
can be chosen and edited too.
.SH NOTES
Programs hear of a change through the X server when they use the X11
backend, and through sockets in
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cgtk
on any backend. A program started without
.I XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
set, on Wayland, sees a new theme only when it is next started.
.SH SEE ALSO
The README and ChangeLog files of CGTK.
